      "writtenOn": "2026-05-28T17:44:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In `updater.cc` (`FtDownloadPage`), you have a very dangerous cast: `std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e(const_cast\u003cuint8_t*\u003e(data.data()), per_len)`\n\nThis happens because `SendAction` and `SendBulkCommand` take a mutable `std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e`, even though an \"OUT\" transfer only reads the data. Forcing a `const_cast` to strip away `const` is a massive red flag in modern C++.\n\n**The Fix:** Split your `SendAction` and `SendBulkCommand` into explicit Read/Write functions, or use overloads so the compiler handles the `const` correctly.\n\n```c\n// Add a const version for OUT transfers\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e SendBulkDataOut(\n    const UsbDevice\u0026 device, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e scsi_cmd,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data_buffer, std::chrono::milliseconds timeout_ms);\n\n// Keep the mutable version for IN transfers\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e ReceiveBulkDataIn(\n    const UsbDevice\u0026 device, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e scsi_cmd,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data_buffer, std::chrono::milliseconds timeout_ms);\n\n```\n\n**The Impact:** You can now pass `std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e` directly to the write functions without ever using `const_cast`.",
